 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gain against current and temperature",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "At VCE = -5 V the gain is close to 340 at 100 °C and 235 at 25 °C at -1 mA, holds nearly flat to some tens of milliamps and then falls, reaching about 190 (100 °C) and 150 (25 °C) at -1 A (Fig.2)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Saturation",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "With base current one tenth of collector current, VCE(sat) sits near -13 to -17 mV below about -10 mA and climbs to roughly -275 to -300 mV at -1 A, the 100 °C curve slightly above the -25 °C one (Fig.3). VBE(sat) under the same drive rises from about -340 mV (100 °C) and -520 mV (-25 °C) at -0.1 mA to about -860 mV and -940 mV at -1 A (Fig.4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Base-emitter on voltage",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "At VCE = -5 V, VBE runs from about -500 mV at -0.1 mA to -820 mV at -1 A at -25 °C, and from about -310 mV to -710 mV at 100 °C (Fig.5)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitance",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "At 1 MHz, Cib falls from about 145 pF at zero bias to 50 pF at 10 V, and Cob from about 58 pF at zero bias to 14 pF at 20 V (Fig.6)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package and assembly",
